{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QuantumObservableUniverses.QSchurWeyl","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QuantumObservableUniverses.QSchurWeyl","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Matrix.Trace","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Algebra.Equiv","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Algebra.Pi","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Group.Finset.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring"],"declarations":10,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.markov_smul","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.markov_smul","kind":"theorem","line":97,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QuantumObservableUniverses/QSchurWeyl.lean#L97-L103","doc":"The Markov trace is `K`-homogeneous. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.markov_add","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.markov_add","kind":"theorem","line":90,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QuantumObservableUniverses/QSchurWeyl.lean#L90-L95","doc":"The Markov trace is additive — it is a genuine trace functional, not an\narbitrary map (consequence of `markov_eq` + linearity of matrix trace). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.markov_one_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.markov_one_eq","kind":"theorem","line":80,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QuantumObservableUniverses/QSchurWeyl.lean#L80-L88","doc":"**Partition function at the identity = `∑_λ d_λ w_λ`.**  The Markov\ntrace of `1` is the degeneracy-weighted sum over Wedderburn blocks — the\n\"total number of states\" of the partition function counted by block, with\n`d_λ` the block dimension.  Proved from the Wedderburn data. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.HeckeWedderburnData.markov_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.HeckeWedderburnData.markov_eq","kind":"theorem","line":75,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QuantumObservableUniverses/QSchurWeyl.lean#L75-L75","doc":"The Markov trace is the weighted character sum `tr_M = ∑_λ w_λ χ_λ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.HeckeWedderburnData.markov","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.HeckeWedderburnData.markov","kind":"def","line":73,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QuantumObservableUniverses/QSchurWeyl.lean#L73-L73","doc":"The Markov trace functional on `H`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.HeckeWedderburnData.iso","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.HeckeWedderburnData.iso","kind":"def","line":71,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QuantumObservableUniverses/QSchurWeyl.lean#L71-L71","doc":"The Wedderburn isomorphism `H ≅ ⊕_λ M_{d_λ}(K)` (Hoefsmit matrix units). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.HeckeWedderburnData.weight","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.HeckeWedderburnData.weight","kind":"def","line":69,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QuantumObservableUniverses/QSchurWeyl.lean#L69-L69","doc":"`w_λ`, the Markov weight of block `λ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.HeckeWedderburnData.dim","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.HeckeWedderburnData.dim","kind":"def","line":67,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QuantumObservableUniverses/QSchurWeyl.lean#L67-L67","doc":"`d_λ = #SYT(λ)`, the dimension of the Specht module `S^λ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.HeckeWedderburnData.mk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.HeckeWedderburnData.mk","kind":"ctor","line":62,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QuantumObservableUniverses/QSchurWeyl.lean#L62-L62","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.HeckeWedderburnData","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.HeckeWedderburnData","kind":"structure","line":55,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QuantumObservableUniverses/QSchurWeyl.lean#L55-L75","doc":"**Quantum Schur–Weyl / Wedderburn data for a Hecke algebra with a\nMarkov trace.**  The fields `iso` and `markov_eq` encode the Jimbo (1986)\ndouble-centralizer theorem together with Hoefsmit's (1974) explicit\npartition-indexed matrix units: `H ≅ ⊕_λ M_{d_λ}(K)` and the Markov trace\nis the weighted character sum `∑_λ w_λ χ_λ`.  A term of this structure is\nthe *hypothesis* \"quantum Schur–Weyl holds for `H`\"; the R2 results below\nare proved from it. ","signatureChunk":0}]}